A||||3||KnightRyder31||Engine runs like crap||||1||
Z||000000||KnightRyder31||09-21-2001||12:56 PM||||When at a complete stop, and then you punch it, the car either stalls or just about stalls. <BR>Also has the ticking noise that I hate(I'm guessing the exhaust manifold).<BR>I have no idea what this stalling and loss of power problem is. I changed the plugs, wires, rotor, cap. So the only thing that I can think of is that it is some sort of a fuel related problem. BTW the car has been doing this every since I bought it. It's a 86 SE 2.5ltr.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Red2m4||09-21-2001||05:32 PM||||<BR>I've had similar problems...<P>Replace plugs and wires and it helped...but not very much.  I noticed that the engine runs much smoother at idle with the A/C off.<P>Did you check for engine codes?  <P>A fuel problem sounds likely.  Perhaps water in the tank?  Filter?  <P>I'm afraid in my case it may be a vacuum leak.<P>Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SLYGUY||09-21-2001||11:38 PM||||My 88 GT runs weird sometimes too. <P>It's the 2.8 V6. <P>Had a lope in the idle. Up to 400rpm when cold. Settles down when warmed up, but still wavers up to 200 rpm then. I figured it was either a sensor, vacuum leak, or both. "Service Engine Soon" light came on after 15 min. or so while driving. <P>In the troubleshooting section of the Haynes manual it diagnosed that symptom as being the EGR valve or solenoid. I ran a diagnostic on the ECU and it displayed a stored code for the EGR. <BR>I replaced it ($30 at Autozone) and that settled it down somewhat. <BR>Still has a crappy idle, pop in the exhaust during upper rpm shifts (5 spd), and coats the rear fascia with soot between washings. <P>Doesn't like to idle with the A/C unless it is warmed up either. I'm guessing that there is another sensor that is affecting both the mixture at idle and upper rpms that is shot.<BR>I'm gonna disconnect the battery for 15 mins. to reset the computer and run it around for a while. Hopefully the check engine light will come on and I can run the diagnostic to find out what other sensor needs replacing. <P>Another small thing that it is doing is puffing a little grey (not white) smoke when you start it after it has sat for at least 15 minutes. It doesn't smoke on a cold startup, but a small amount of black smoke when it is running indicating a rich mixture.<P>The plugs and distributor cap/rotor have not been changed yet. Spark plug wires look good however.<P>Between me, the Chilton's and Haynes manuals I'll get it purring. <P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||SLYGUY||09-22-2001||02:39 AM||||I am an IDIOT!!!<P>Well, not that bad but listen...<P>I replaced the EGR and thought that by doing that by itself would cure the mixture/idle lope problem. <BR>Thats the first step but I forgot one very important thing.<P>RESET THE ECU!!<P>I read in the Haynes manual that you have to disconnect the battery (ign. off) and reconnect 10-15 seconds later to clear the codes and reset the ECU.<P>I did this, she stumbled momentarily and now purrs like a kitten!<P>Idle is not purrfect, but no longer lopes in search of the EGR to fine tune. With a fresh set of plugs and new cap and rotor you will be able to set a watch to it!!<P>I'm stoked!! Like a kid with a new toy!!<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SLYGUYSIG.jpg"> ||||reg||1
